        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/47647?u=2fbfc431a85d357f00c0146d5e55b425b7f00a3d&v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/>\n<h4 dir=\"auto\">Summary</h4>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Chrome 136 introduces the CSS <a href=\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color-hdr/#the-dynamic-range-limit-property\" rel=\"nofollow\"><code class=\"notranslate\">dynamic-range-limit</code></a> property; see <a href=\"https://chromestatus.com/feature/5146250411769856\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://chromestatus.com/feature/5146250411769856</a>. This already had BCD.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I'm pretty sure this support also includes the <code class=\"notranslate\">dynamic-range-limit-mix()</code> function, but I've asked Chrome engineering to clarify. I've already been told that the function is Chrome-only at the moment.</p>\n\n<h4 dir=\"auto\">Test results and supporting details</h4>\n\n\n<h4 dir=\"auto\">Related issues</h4>\n\n",
